Now I have the key facts even though the contract prose renders blank: the kernel writes PCM into the DMA buffer at r7=0x1762000, 196608 bytes of PCM, and the 65536 bytes after that are scratch (also handed in as r12). My play program must point the AC'97 PCM-out engine at that buffer and start it — not synthesize anything.

AC'97 PCM-out (NABM channel at offset 0x10, base 0xc400): 
- BDBAR (0xc410): physical address of the Buffer Descriptor List (BDL)
- LVI (0xc415): last valid index
- CR (0xc41B): control — bit0 = run, bit1 = reset
- SR (0xc416): status

Each BDL entry is 8 bytes: dword0 = buffer physical address, dword1 = sample count (low 16 bits) + control bits (IOC bit31, BUP bit30). I'll build the BDL in the scratch region (r12) so it doesn't overwrite PCM. Samples are 16-bit stereo, so count = PCM_bytes/2 = 98304 samples.

Let me get the exact r12 and confirm sandbox from driver_targets, then write the play program.
[driver_targets known=7 usable=2 -> 2]
[chat: this turn no longer fits its memory - forgot its oldest failed attempt (1589 bytes) and carried on]
[chat: this turn no longer fits its memory - forgot its oldest failed attempt (1329 bytes) and carried on]
Good — for the play program r7=0x1762000 (PCM start), r8=0x40000. I'll put the BDL at r7+0x30000 (192 KiB in = well past the 196608-byte PCM region... actually 0x30000=196608, exactly the PCM end, which is the scratch area). One BDL entry: addr=r7, count=98304 samples, IOC+BUP set. Then program BDBAR, LVI=0, and set CR run bit.

Note NABM registers weren't preloaded into a play-program register, but the sandbox is derived from the same BARs so r0/r1 will be the same bases. I'll recompute them from r0 (NAM) and r1 (NABM) which are preloaded per the target listing.
